WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:05.859
"The Witcher" games, and the ultra-popular
"The Witcher III: The Wild Hunt" in particular,

00:00:05.859 --> 00:00:12.560
have given many fans a very definitive depiction
of Andrzej Sapkowski's mysterious Continent

00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:15.090
and its many strange denizens.

00:00:15.090 --> 00:00:20.410
As such, Netflix's live-action adaptation
of "The Witcher" was always going to be a

00:00:20.410 --> 00:00:21.970
tough balancing act.

00:00:21.970 --> 00:00:26.820
How to recreate a world that millions of players
have already obsessed over for countless hours

00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:28.609
in a familiar, yet fresh way?

00:00:28.609 --> 00:00:33.670
How to create the sense of danger that the
game's many powerful enemies and monsters

00:00:33.670 --> 00:00:34.670
radiate?

00:00:34.670 --> 00:00:40.620
And how, oh how, could anyone hope to capture
the gruff energy of the main character, Geralt

00:00:40.620 --> 00:00:41.629
of Rivia?

00:00:41.629 --> 00:00:47.010
Though he's best known for his comparatively
clean-cut turn as the DC Extended Universe

00:00:47.010 --> 00:00:52.339
version of Superman, Cavill absolutely nails
the essence of Geralt.

00:00:52.339 --> 00:00:58.230
The actor prepared extensively for the role,
transforming his body to better suit its needs

00:00:58.230 --> 00:01:02.250
and, of course, delivering his lines with
that signature growl.

00:01:02.250 --> 00:01:07.720
However, he has now revealed that one important
aspect in his character work happened completely

00:01:07.720 --> 00:01:08.740
by accident.

00:01:08.740 --> 00:01:12.500
Yeah, that iconic voice Cavill uses in "The
Witcher?"

00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:18.299
As he revealed during CD PROJECT RED and Netflix's
virtual WitcherCon, it was a total mistake

00:01:18.299 --> 00:01:19.450
on the actor's part.

00:01:19.450 --> 00:01:24.020
He simply forgot what kind of voice he was
supposed to use partway through the shoot,

00:01:24.020 --> 00:01:28.630
and after he heard what he sounded like in
a scene with King Foltest, he was delighted

00:01:28.630 --> 00:01:32.979
— but also slightly worried, because he
initially felt the delivery was a bit too

00:01:32.979 --> 00:01:37.810
close to voice actor Doug Cockle's performance
in "The Witcher" games.

00:01:37.810 --> 00:01:38.810
Cavill said,

00:01:38.810 --> 00:01:41.590
"I know it's very similar to Doug Cockle's
amazing work.

00:01:41.590 --> 00:01:43.509
That was definitely a concern of mine.

00:01:43.509 --> 00:01:47.990
I thought, 'Well, I don't want to seem like
I'm plagiarizing another professional's extraordinary

00:01:47.990 --> 00:01:48.990
work.'

00:01:48.990 --> 00:01:52.909
And I had a look at it, had a listen to it,
and I thought, 'actually, they are different

00:01:52.909 --> 00:01:53.909
enough.

00:01:53.909 --> 00:01:57.439
It's clearly inspired by [Cockle's version]
— but I did my own thing.'"

00:01:57.439 --> 00:02:01.469
There was just one problem: They had already
shot some scenes for the show.

00:02:01.469 --> 00:02:06.909
Fortunately, the powers that be liked Cavill's
new take on the voice so much that they offered

00:02:06.909 --> 00:02:08.560
to reshoot the scenes.

00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:13.540
Seeing as the combination of Geralt's harsh
voice, strange accent, and quiet nature play

00:02:13.540 --> 00:02:18.360
a large part in his characterization, it's
probably fair to say that this was the right

00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:19.360
call.

00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:24.310
Series creator Lauren Schmidt Hissrich knew
that the role of Geralt would be one quite

00:02:24.310 --> 00:02:28.870
sought after, but she also knew that she was
going for a certain iconic voice to bring

00:02:28.870 --> 00:02:31.000
the character to the screen.

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:32.409
Hissrich told us here at Looper,

00:02:32.409 --> 00:02:36.849
"At the end of the day, though, I realized
that I actually kind of had Henry Cavill's

00:02:36.849 --> 00:02:41.500
voice in my head, and I called him and explained
basically this whole thing."

00:02:41.500 --> 00:02:46.970
So although he found his Geralt voice by a
happy accident, the actor's voice was a factor

00:02:46.970 --> 00:02:49.480
before he was even officially cast for the
show!

00:02:49.480 --> 00:02:54.810
Nevertheless, Cavill refused to outright accept
the part, as he shared Hissrich's philosophy

00:02:54.810 --> 00:02:58.680
that the production should seek far and wide
for its Geralt.

00:02:58.680 --> 00:02:59.680
Hissrich said,

00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:04.090
"And he was like, 'Great, if you want me,
I want you to know that I'm the right person.

00:03:04.090 --> 00:03:06.470
I want you to have seen everyone and met everyone.

00:03:06.470 --> 00:03:09.989
And we met up again and we and talked for
several hours.

00:03:09.989 --> 00:03:14.519
And by the time we left that day, I knew that
I had found our Geralt."

00:03:14.519 --> 00:03:19.860
In the end, Cavill ended up coming out on
top in a casting process that Hissrich said

00:03:19.860 --> 00:03:25.519
involved "207 potential Geralts" — and that
was before the actor even found the character's

00:03:25.519 --> 00:03:26.850
iconic voice.

00:03:26.850 --> 00:03:28.620
Not bad!

